depends: stop sbs-cleanup from deleting the source cache.

sbs-cleanup runs between targets to free the intermediate build trees,
and its last line is `rm -rf simplybs/.buildlib/source`. That directory is
not an intermediate: it is the download cache, keyed by URL path, and every
entry in it has already been checked against the sha256 pinned in the
package JSON. Removing it makes each of the ten targets re-download the
whole source set from scratch, which is where most of the wall-clock time
and every network flake in this build comes from -- the llvm-project bundle
alone is 2.2 GiB.

It is also load we have no right to put on GNU and the other origins ten
times over, and it is what makes the Apple targets impossible to build at
all. native/apple-sdk cannot fetch its Xcode xip (Apple 302s to a developer
login), so the xip has to be staged into that cache by hand; deleting the
cache after the first target throws the staged xip away with it.

The other six lines are left alone. Those do remove real intermediates:
work/ and staging/ trees, the extracted per-triplet prefixes, and _native.

Keeping the cache does hand one failure mode a longer life: DownloadSource
reuses whatever file is already at a cache path without re-checking it, so
a run killed mid-transfer leaves a truncated archive that sbs-cleanup used
to sweep away and now nothing does. Patch 0011 closes that, and the two
belong together.

--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -21,7 +21,6 @@
 	@rm -rf *-*-*
 	@rm -rf simplybs/.buildlib/*_*/work
 	@rm -rf simplybs/.buildlib/*_*/staging
-	@rm -rf simplybs/.buildlib/source
 
 simplybs:
 	git clone https://github.com/mrcyjanek/simplybs || true
